# Bank 2 (runs at 0x8000). Directives: L label, C line comment,
# B block header, D data range, F row width.
#
# THE BANK STARTS IN THE MIDDLE OF AN INSTRUCTION. Its first byte, the 0x80 at
# 0x8000, is not the start of anything: it is the third byte of the
# `ld hl,0x8041` that starts at 0x7FFE of bank 1. Execution enters here at
# 0x8001, without any jump. It is the only place in the cartridge where an
# instruction is split between two banks, and it welds bank 1 to bank 2: it
# only works with the bank layout that INIT sets up.
#
# Something similar but milder happens at the bottom end: the code falls
# through from 0x9FFF into 0xA000, that is, into bank 3.
B 0x8000 THE FIRST BYTE IS THE THIRD BYTE OF AN INSTRUCTION FROM BANK 1
D 0x8000 0x8001 third_byte_of_split_instruction  The 0x80 that completes the `ld hl,0x8041` at p01:7FFE. It is listed separately because there is no instruction in this bank that contains it.
F 0x8000 1
B 0x8001 THE BANK OF THE BOSSES AND OF COLLISIONS WITH THE MAP
B 0x8001 This bank holds the mover routines of the stage bosses, the routine that
B 0x8001 says whether a point collides with the map, and a good part of the
B 0x8001 drawing. It starts in an odd way: the first instruction is NOT entirely
B 0x8001 here; its first two bytes are the last ones of bank 1.
L 0x8001 continued_from_bank_1  This is where the split instruction that starts at p01:7FFE continues.
C 0x8001 HL arrives here from bank 1
C 0x8004 The byte is kept in the alternate C
C 0x8006 0xE790 and 0xE7C0: if the boss is already set up, it is not set up again
C 0x8013 Ten bytes from 0x8051 to the card
C 0x8021 Bit 0 of C: the first piece
C 0x802F And bit 1: the second
C 0x803B Twelve bytes of card
D 0x8041 0x8073 table_8041  Fifty bytes pointed to by the `ld hl,0x8041` of the split instruction: it is the first thing the code loads on entering this bank.
F 0x8041 16
L 0x8073 run_five_at_e7a0   The five slots at 0xE7A0; only the type 4 ones do anything
C 0x8077 Five slots
C 0x807D Type 4
C 0x8083 Sixteen bytes: the next slot
L 0x808B fire_piece_shot   Every so many frames (fewer the harder it is) fires a shot from wherever the table at 0x80B0 says
C 0x808F 0x1B minus the difficulty, divided by two: the frames between shots
C 0x809D The table at 0x80B0: where each drawing's shot comes out from
C 0x80AD And bank 1 sets it up
D 0x80B0 0x8100 table_80B0  Eighty bytes read by 0x809D.
F 0x80B0 16
L 0x8100 run_six_pieces   The six slots at 0xE790: each with its mover routine according to its type
C 0x8104 Six slots
C 0x810B Sixteen bytes: the next slot
L 0x8113 piece_step   Types 3 and up: 3 withdraws, and the others turn towards the drawing that is due to them
C 0x8116 Below type 3 it does nothing
C 0x8119 Type 3 withdraws
C 0x811F The R register: five to eight frames per step
C 0x812A Byte 10 is the current drawing and 11 the one it wants to reach
C 0x8135 One step towards it, up or down
C 0x8139 The ramp at 0x81AD: the real drawing
L 0x8144 withdraw_piece   It keeps rising and moving away from the ship, changing drawing, until it leaves through the top
C 0x8147 Bit 4 of the counter: alternates the drawing
C 0x8153 From step 2 onwards, without alternating
C 0x8162 Ten frames per step
C 0x8169 Eight points higher
C 0x816B Above Y 0x10 it is over
C 0x8178 0xE204: towards where the ship is
C 0x817E Byte 32 says how far it can go
C 0x8188 Eight to the right
C 0x818A Eight to the right
C 0x818B With a limit on the right
C 0x8199 Or eight to the left
C 0x819B Byte 16: how far it gets
C 0x819C With a limit
L 0x81A2 switch_off_piece   The slot and its two marks, zeroed
D 0x81AD 0x81D5 ramp_81AD  Forty bytes read by 0x8139: a downward slope (0x27, 0x26, 0x25, ...) followed by an upward slope.
F 0x81AD 16
